Description: RAM guard that freezes the program filling the memory
 Gagarine keeps a Linux machine from freezing when its memory fills up. It
 measures the RAM every second (four times per second above 85 %). At 95 %
 it freezes (SIGSTOP) the program that grows the fastest, throttles it (if
 resumed, it can only grow by a small amount), pushes it into swap, then
 stops it if the RAM does not go down once the swap-out is done, if the RAM
 reaches 98 %, or if it stays frozen for 10 minutes.
 .
 It runs as a system service (root), never touches the desktop or critical
 services, and sends desktop notifications offering to stop or resume the
 frozen program. The "gagarine essai" command checks the whole chain safely.
 .
 It also sets up a compressed swap in RAM (zram, the size of the RAM, zstd,
 used before the disk swap) with matching kernel settings, so that frozen
 programs are pushed out fast and the machine never thrashes the disk.
 .
 It includes a queue for heavy jobs ("gagarine file"): jobs run one at a
 time, each one locked into a kernel memory limit, so a runaway job cannot
 take the machine down.
 .
 A GNOME graphical interface ("gagarine-interface", GTK 4 and libadwaita)
 shows the RAM, the swap, the frozen programs, the job queue, the journal
 and the settings, and drives the service. A panel indicator
 ("gagarine-indicateur") shows the RAM in the top bar at all times. Both are
 optional: they need the recommended packages.
 .
 Complements systemd-oomd and earlyoom.

